    "result_text": "The full suite (the project's only test class) is green: `Tests run: 3, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` and `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented address normalization applied when an owner is created, in `OwnerRestControllerV1.java`.\n\n**What changed:**\n\n1. **Added a `normalizeAddress` helper** that: trims, collapses runs of whitespace to a single space, upper-cases, and expands the abbreviations `ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE` (token-by-token so only standalone abbreviations are expanded). A `null`/all-whitespace input normalizes to `\"\"`.\n\n2. **Wired it into `addOwner`**: right after email normalization the address is normalized and written back onto the DTO, so the normalized form is what gets persisted, returned (`$.address`), and used in every downstream comparison. If the address is blank after normalization, the create is rejected with `400 Bad Request`.\n\n3. **Comparisons use the normalized form automatically**: because the normalized value is stored back on the DTO before the household-duplicate check runs (and is what gets saved), both the duplicate detection in the controller and the `householdId` derivation in `OwnerMapper` now operate on the normalized address. Owners created via the endpoint all store the canonical form, so `normalizeHouseholdKey` comparisons are consistent and idempotent.\n\n**Behavior verified by the new acceptance tests:**\n- `\"  12  main  st \"` \u2192 stored/returned as `\"12 MAIN STREET\"`\n- `\"7 elm ave\"` \u2192 `\"7 ELM AVENUE\"`\n- `\"   \"` \u2192 `400 Bad Request`\n\nOne thing I noticed: the generated `OwnerFieldsDto` already carries a Bean Validation `Pattern (.*\\S.*)` on `address`, which independently rejects all-whitespace input at 400. The explicit post-normalization check keeps the rejection guaranteed at the business-rule layer as the spec requires, and the two agree (normalization can only make a non-blank value blank if it was all whitespace, which the pattern also rejects).",
